Google Cloud Platform (GCP) is Google's suite of cloud computing services, offering on-demand access to infrastructure like computing power, storage, networking, databases, big data analytics, and machine learning tools, all running on Google's global, secure, and highly scalable infrastructure, similar to what powers Gmail and YouTube. It provides Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS), Platform as a Service (PaaS), and serverless options for building, deploying, and managing applications, helping businesses reduce costs and scale operations without managing physical hardware. 
Key Aspects of GCP
Services: Offers modular services for Compute Engine, Kubernetes Engine (GKE), Cloud Storage, BigQuery, AI/ML (Vertex AI), networking, and more.
Infrastructure: Leverages Google's worldwide network of data centers, regions, and zones for high availability and low latency.
Technology: Built on the same technology that runs Google's own services, ensuring reliability and performance.
Benefits: Scalability, cost-effectiveness (pay-per-second billing), strong security, and seamless integration.
Use Cases: Web/app hosting, data analytics, AI/ML model training, data storage, and enterprise management. 
GCP vs. Google Cloud
Google Cloud is the broader offering, including GCP, Google Workspace (Docs, Gmail), and Google Maps Platform.
GCP specifically refers to the core public cloud infrastructure and services for developers and businesses. 


